= Templates and categories =

Templates and categories solve different problems.

* '''Template''' = reusable/transcluded declarative content, structure, semantic emission, or stable invocation surface.
* '''Module / Lua''' = reusable non-trivial normalization, validation, bounded inference, computation, semantic presentation, or diagnostics.
* '''Category''' = human-readable grouping and automatic index/navigation surface.
* '''Property / SMW''' = durable semantic attribute or relationship.
* '''Cargo''' = repeated structured operational records when a table-shaped lifecycle is justified.
* '''Book Matter''' = reusable content anatomy.
* '''Special page / validation category''' = generated operational state.

Do not use one merely because another would require more thought.

== V2 composition path ==

<pre>
wikitext author
    ↓
Template
    ├── declarative rendering / transclusion
    ├── SMW property emission
    ├── Cargo operational storage where explicitly designed
    └── #invoke → Scribunto/Lua for reusable procedural logic
                         ↓
           normalized / validated / computed projection
</pre>

Templates are the public wikitext-facing contract. Lua is the deterministic compiler/projection layer when procedure becomes non-trivial. SMW and Cargo remain state owners; a Template or Lua module should not create a hidden competing schema.

<code>Template:Knowledge object</code> demonstrates this split: it exposes the authoring contract, invokes <code>Module:Structure</code>, and that wrapper delegates normalization/type checking to <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code> using the runtime schema projection.


== Canonical V2 template rules ==
A V2 template should have:
# a clear reusable purpose;
# documented parameters;
# one minimal usage example;
# explicit dependencies where relevant;
# source/provenance notes when inherited material or transformations matter;
# template documentation/categories inside <code>&lt;noinclude&gt;</code>;
# transcluding behavior inside <code>&lt;includeonly&gt;</code> only when that behavior belongs on caller pages.

Use templates as pattern languages: '''consistency without rigidity'''. A template should make repeated structure easier to understand and maintain; it should not force unrelated knowledge into one shape.

When logic becomes procedural or reused across several presentation contexts, prefer a small <code>Module:BITwiki/*</code> function returning structured normalized state rather than deeply nested parser functions.

== Canonical V2 category rules ==
A created V2 Category page should state, when useful:
* what it groups;
* inclusion/exclusion criteria;
* parent category/categories;
* whether it is a domain, navigation group, project/meta grouping or generated operational category.

Category pages are indexes, not directories. A page may belong to multiple categories. Category hierarchy is a navigation graph, not the ontology.

A category may include SMW/query/Lua-computed views, but those are projections over canonical state. Category membership does not silently create Entity type, Domain, or relationship assertions.

== State and rendering invariants ==

<pre>
Template invocation
≠ independent semantic authority

Lua-derived display
≠ stored SMW assertion

Category membership
≠ ontology inheritance

Cargo operational record
≠ canonical knowledge object
</pre>

== Maintenance surfaces ==
These are generated reports, not ordinary content pages:
* [[Special:AllPages]] — enumerate pages by namespace;
* [[Special:Categories]] — used categories;
* [[Special:UncategorizedPages]] — ordinary pages without category tags;
* [[Special:UncategorizedTemplates]] — templates without category tags;
* [[Special:UnusedTemplates]] — templates with no current transclusion caller;
* [[Special:WantedTemplates]] — referenced templates that do not exist;
* [[Special:WantedCategories]] — used categories without an authored Category page;
* [[Special:UnusedCategories]] — created category pages with no members;
* [[Special:BrokenRedirects]] / [[Special:DoubleRedirects]] — redirect hygiene.


== Release checks ==
Before a deployment is considered clean:
# verify current Template pages against the current live wiki;
# review [[Special:UncategorizedTemplates]], [[Special:UnusedTemplates]] and [[Special:WantedTemplates]];
# review [[Special:Categories]], [[Special:WantedCategories]] and [[Special:UncategorizedPages]];
# verify semantic properties and category membership after template changes;
# verify Lua module dependency order and actual Scribunto behavior for invoked modules;
# verify redirects/transclusions after every rename;
# retire compatibility templates only after current callers and rendered behavior are verified.


See [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]], [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]], [[BITwiki:MediaWiki substrate]], [[BITwiki:Transclusion]], and [[BITwiki:Contributing]].

